E=[]
def get_source(needle):
 for fn in sorted(BASE.glob('root_*.json')):
  rows=json.loads(fn.read_text())
  if not isinstance(rows,list):continue
  for i,r in enumerate(rows):
   for c in r.get('result',{}).get('content',[]):
    if c.get('type')=='text':
     for line in c['text'].splitlines():
      if (line.startswith('- ') or line.startswith('[PRIOR')) and needle in line:return line,f'{fn.name} record {i}'
 raise ValueError(needle)
def add(title,needle,field,question,summary,definitions,statement,proof,setup,calculation,conclusion,limits,extension,refs):
 s,loc=get_source(needle);tag=re.search(r'([UA])@(2026-06-\d\d)',s)
 ev,date=(tag.group(1),tag.group(2)) if tag else ('T',None)
 E.append(dict(id=f'Y26-06-{len(E)+1:03d}',title=title,status='Classical theorem or newly stipulated exact model; speculative source assertions delimited',summary=summary,definitions=definitions,result=dict(name=title,statement=statement,proof=proof),example=dict(setup=setup,calculation=calculation,conclusion=conclusion),limits=limits,extension=extension,references=[dict(label=k,url=REF[k]) for k in refs],original=dict(date=date,month='2026-06',evidence=ev,field=field,concept=title,origin='Recovered dated mathematical fragment; external file evidence is retained as T',question='Reconstruction: '+question,math=statement,finish='Precise hypotheses, proof or derivation, worked example and finite certificate',kind='Substantial mathematical redevelopment',refs=[REF[k] for k in refs],source_excerpt=s,retrieval_receipt=loc,original_question=None,attribution_note='The source excerpt is an exact retrieved summary line, not a claimed verbatim historical prompt. Revised questions, proofs and stipulated examples are newly authored. U/A preserve retrieved authorship; T denotes external context with no invented conversation date. Source numerical results and software test counts are not independently certified here.')))

add('Finite Weyl pairs exist exactly at root-of-unity phases','Original source equation','Noncommutative algebra','When does UV=qVU admit invertible finite matrices, and how can one construct an irreducible model?',
'The source’s central rational/irrational phase distinction has a short exact proof. The finite construction below explains conductor dimension and the full matrix algebra generated by the clock and shift, without identifying a finite approximation with the irrational operator algebra.',
['Let q∈C× and U,V be invertible d-by-d matrices satisfying UV=qVU. If q has exact order L, use basis e_j, j modulo L, and define Ue_j=q^j e_j, Ve_j=e_{j+1}.','A representation is irreducible if it has no common nonzero proper invariant subspace. A commutant consists of matrices commuting with both generators.'],
'Finite invertible solutions force q^d=1. For q of order L, the displayed L-dimensional unitary pair satisfies the relation and generates all L-by-L matrices; its commutant is the scalars and its representation is irreducible.',
'Taking determinants gives det(U)det(V)=q^d det(V)det(U). Invertibility permits cancellation, proving the necessary root-of-unity condition and L|d. The displayed pair has UV e_j=q^{j+1}e_{j+1}=qVU e_j, including wraparound because q^L=1. The eigenvalues of U are distinct. Lagrange interpolation in U therefore produces each coordinate projection E_jj. Multiplying by powers of V produces every matrix unit E_ij, so the generated algebra is the full matrix algebra. Any invariant subspace is then invariant under every matrix unit and is either zero or the whole space. A matrix commuting with U is diagonal; commuting additionally with V forces all its diagonal entries equal. This proves all claims without invoking a classification of arbitrary representations. When q=e^{2πiθ} with irrational θ, q^d≠1 for every positive d, so no exact finite invertible pair exists.',
'Take q=e^{2πi/3} and dimension three.',
'U=diag(1,q,q²), and V cyclically permutes the coordinates. The nine products U^aV^b, 0≤a,b<3, are Hilbert–Schmidt orthogonal because the trace of a nontrivial shift is zero and the trace of a nontrivial clock power is1+q+q²=0. Thus they form a basis of M₃(C).',
'The finite conductor and the irrational obstruction are exact algebraic facts.',
'Approximate commutation at a rational approximant is not an exact representation at irrational θ. Rational phase does not by itself identify a categorical realization, gate cost or an infinite Euler product.',
'Given rational approximants to an irrational θ, derive a norm bound for commutation defects and specify which observables converge, keeping finite matrix approximation distinct from exact representation.',['Weil'])

add('Quadratic polarization is symmetric and cannot equal an alternating form at odd order','Proposed finite phase modules','Finite quadratic forms','Which bilinear form can a quadratic refinement polarize, and what must be corrected in a symplectic-looking source definition?',
'The source writes q(x+y)−q(x)−q(y)=ω(x,y) for a finite phase module. The left side is symmetric in x and y. At odd order it therefore cannot be a nonzero alternating form. This elementary obstruction is foundational for choosing consistent Weil and Heisenberg data.',
['Let A be an abelian group and Q:A→Q/Z with Q(0)=0. Define b_Q(x,y)=Q(x+y)−Q(x)−Q(y). A quadratic form additionally has bilinear b_Q and Q(nx)=n²Q(x).','An alternating form ω has ω(x,x)=0 and ω(y,x)=−ω(x,y). The Heisenberg commutator form and a quadratic module’s symmetric bilinear form play different roles.'],
'b_Q is always symmetric. If b_Q is also alternating and A has odd order, then b_Q=0. For A=Z/pZ with odd prime p, Q_a(x)=ax²/p mod1 has b_Q(x,y)=2axy/p and is nondegenerate exactly when p∤a.',
'Symmetry follows immediately from commutativity of x+y. If a form is symmetric and skew-symmetric, then2b(x,y)=0. For finite odd-order A, each b(x,y) has odd order dividing the order of x, so it cannot have order two unless it is zero. This proves the obstruction. The cyclic example is well defined because replacing x by x+p changes ax²/p by2ax+ap, an integer. Expansion gives the displayed polarization. If p∤a and b_Q(x,y)=0 for every y, choose y=1; then p divides2ax, and oddness implies x=0. If p|a the form is zero. The same calculation shows why Q(x)=x²/(2L) must be assigned a correct underlying cyclic group and parity condition rather than copied to any Z/LZ: changing x by L contributes x+L/2, which is integral only when L is even.',
'Use A=Z/3Z and Q(x)=x²/3.',
'Its values are0,1/3,1/3, and b(1,1)=2/3≠0, so it is nondegenerate symmetric but not alternating. In contrast the standard form on F₃², ω((x,y),(x′,y′))=(xy′−yx′)/3, is alternating and nonzero; it cannot be the polarization of an ordinary scalar quadratic Q on that group.',
'The source’s two structures are separated by an exact impossibility result and a valid replacement.',
'Characteristic two and groups with two-torsion require different quadratic-refinement conventions. This theorem does not deny those theories; it states why the odd-order identification fails.',
'Define the Heisenberg extension from its alternating commutator and the Weil representation from its symmetric quadratic module separately, then prove any relationship through a chosen symplectic module and metaplectic action.',['Weil'])

add('Finite Fourier and Gauss operators satisfy a projective modular relation with a computed phase','Finite quadratic module machinery','Weil representation','What S and T conventions give the finite modular relations, including their Gauss phase?',
'The source proposes S²=C and (ST)³=ξC. We prove these equations for the unnormalized-by-signature finite operators, so the phase is computed rather than hidden. A genuine representation may require a metaplectic lift or a compensating scalar.',
['Let (A,Q) be a finite quadratic module with nondegenerate symmetric polarization b. On C[A] set S_xy=|A|^{−1/2}e^{−2πib(x,y)} and T_xx=e^{2πiQ(x)}.','Let C send e_x to e_{−x}, and let γ=|A|^{−1/2}Σ_xe^{2πiQ(x)}. These conventions omit a possible signature multiplier on S.'],
'S is unitary, S²=C and (ST)³=γC. For A=Z/3Z,Q(x)=x²/3, γ=i. Direct sums multiply γ and tensor the S,T operators.',
'Character orthogonality gives Σ_y e^{−2πib(x+z,y)}=|A| when z=−x and zero otherwise, proving S²=C and unitarity. Complete the square in the quadratic Gauss sum: Q(y)−b(y,a)=Q(y−a)−Q(a). Thus Σ_y e^{2πi(Q(y)−b(y,a))}=√|A|γ e^{−2πiQ(a)}. In the matrix product (ST)², take a=x+z to obtain γ|A|^{−1/2}e^{2πi(Q(z)−Q(x+z))}. Multiplying once more by ST leaves an orthogonality sum supported at the final index−x; the remaining Q terms cancel and yield γC. Since ST is unitary, the equation also forces |γ|=1. Under orthogonal direct sum the exponential and finite sum factor, proving the tensor and phase multiplication laws.',
'For the order-three module put ζ=e^{2πi/3}.',
'T=diag(1,ζ,ζ), b(x,y)=2xy/3, and γ=(1+2ζ)/√3=i. Therefore (ST)³=iC rather than C. Squaring S reverses the labels1 and2. The numerical verifier checks all entries of both identities to a declared floating tolerance; the preceding finite-sum argument is their proof.',
'The modular relation now has explicit normalization and an auditable central phase.',
'A finite pair of matrices satisfying projective relations is not automatically the fusion or associator data of an arbitrary proposed category. Coherence, positivity and categorical realization remain separate inputs.',
'Normalize S by a signature phase in the appropriate metaplectic representation, decompose prime-power modules exactly and verify compatibility of their local Gauss factors under CRT.',['Weil'])

add('CRT factorization and idempotent splitting give constructive finite closure','c(p^a)=a+1','Conductor arithmetic and algebra','Which parts of conductor block counts are arithmetic facts, and what does idempotent completion actually add?',
'The source reports four blocks at161 and eight at483, alongside idempotent/Karoubi closure. We prove the arithmetic count and the linear-algebraic splitting mechanism, while distinguishing them from an unprovided theorem that a particular compiler has exactly those blocks.',
['For L=∏p_i^{a_i}, the divisor count is τ(L)=∏(a_i+1). The Chinese remainder theorem gives Z/LZ≅∏Z/p_i^{a_i}Z.','An idempotent linear map P on V satisfies P²=P. Its split object is imP, with inclusion i and projection p such that pi=I and ip=P.'],
'τ(161)=4 and τ(483)=8. CRT produces orthogonal idempotents summing to one for the coprime factors. Every finite-dimensional idempotent splits as V=imP⊕kerP, and the image has dimension trP.',
'A divisor independently chooses an exponent between zero and a_i, proving the count. For coprime m,n choose inverses n^{−1} modm and m^{−1} modn. Then e_m=n(n^{−1} modm) is one modm and zero modn; e_n is the opposite. Their products and squares are checked componentwise by CRT, yielding e_m+e_n=1, e_me_n=0 and e_i²=e_i. For linear P, write v=Pv+(v−Pv); the second summand is killed by P, and a vector in both image and kernel is zero because P acts as identity on its image. In a basis adapted to this direct sum P is diag(I_r,0), giving trace r. Idempotent completion formally adjoins such image objects where an abstract category did not already contain them. It does not change the underlying polynomial identity P²=P into a physical measurement theorem.',
'For161=7·23, use e_7=92 and e_23=70 modulo161.',
'We have92≡1 mod7 and0 mod23;70≡0 mod7 and1 mod23; their sum162≡1 and their product6440=40·161. Its divisors are1,7,23,161. Adding the factor3 gives483 and eight divisors. For P=[[1,1],[0,0]], P²=P, image span(e₁), kernel span((-1,1)), and trace one.',
'The exact arithmetic and image-splitting steps are constructive and separately testable.',
'The equality c(L)=τ(L) is a definition or source model unless the actual conductor algebra is proved to decompose by divisors. It cannot independently establish a gate-count speedup.',
'For the specified conductor representation, construct all central projectors, prove they are complete and orthogonal, and calculate each nonzero block’s rank before asserting its compiler cost.',['Number fields','Weil'])

add('SELECT plus state preparation gives an exact linear-combination block','Weighted SELECT/compiler formulas','Quantum algorithms','What finite identity underlies a structured SELECT construction before any runtime estimate is claimed?',
'The source proposes structured conductor SELECT and reports modeled speedups. The mathematical core of linear combinations of unitaries can be established exactly. It exposes the needed normalization λ and the distinction between a block identity and a complete simulation complexity theorem.',
['Let H=Σ_{j=0}^{m−1}a_jU_j with a_j≥0 and unitary U_j. Set λ=Σa_j>0 and prepare |a⟩=Σ√(a_j/λ)|j⟩.','Define SELECT=Σ|j⟩⟨j|⊗U_j. If PREP|0⟩=|a⟩, let W=(PREP†⊗I)SELECT(PREP⊗I).'],
'W is unitary and its ancilla-zero block is (⟨0|⊗I)W(|0⟩⊗I)=H/λ. In particular ||H||≤λ. Postselecting ancilla zero on input ψ succeeds with probability ||Hψ||²/λ².',
'SELECT is block diagonal with unitary blocks, so it is unitary; multiplication by the preparation unitaries preserves this. Insert the expansion of |a⟩ on both sides of SELECT. Orthogonality of the label basis removes all cross terms, leaving Σ(a_j/λ)U_j. This proves the exact block identity. A subblock of a unitary is a contraction, yielding the norm inequality; it also follows from the triangle inequality. The ancilla-zero component of the output is Hψ/λ, whose squared norm is exactly the stated probability. Complex or negative coefficients can be included by absorbing their phases into the corresponding U_j. A structured implementation may reduce the cost of SELECT or PREP, but does not alter this normalization, and the complete cost depends on both operations.',
'Take H=2X+Z on a qubit, with Pauli X and Z.',
'Here λ=3, |a⟩=√(2/3)|0⟩+√(1/3)|1⟩, and the block is(2X+Z)/3. Since XZ+ZX=0, H²=5I, so the success probability is5/9 for every normalized input. H has norm√5, strictly smaller than the coefficient sum three. This gap shows that λ is a construction parameter rather than automatically the exact spectral norm.',
'The source compiler has a concrete algebraic target and normalization check.',
'No2.5–5× speedup follows from the identity. A Hamiltonian simulation theorem additionally needs its approximation method, oracle model, precision and costs of PREP, SELECT and controlled inverses.',
'For the conductor decomposition, implement the exact block structure, count gates under one explicit cost model and bound coefficient-loading overhead before comparing with a baseline implementation.',['LCU'])

add('Sequential quantum instruments preserve probability only when every branch is included','instrument composition','Quantum measurement','Which equations certify a branch-resolved measurement process and its composition?',
'The June QRAM work emphasizes measurement-native operations and branch tensors. A finite Kraus construction gives the exact compositional theorem and explains why analyzing one postselected branch is insufficient for a complete fault or runtime claim.',
['An instrument has branch maps Φ_a(ρ)=Σ_rK_{ar}ρK_{ar}† with Σ_{a,r}K_{ar}†K_{ar}=I. Branch probability is trΦ_a(ρ), and normalized conditional state divides by that probability when nonzero.','A later instrument may depend on a, with operators L_{bs|a} satisfying Σ_{b,s}L_{bs|a}†L_{bs|a}=I for every a.'],
'The composed branches have Kraus operators L_{bs|a}K_{ar}. Summing all branches is trace preserving. Ignoring or renormalizing away failed branches changes the process and can conceal arbitrarily small success probability.',
'Complete positivity of each branch follows directly from the Kraus form, including after adjoining any untouched auxiliary system. For completeness, sum(LK)†(LK) over b,s first; the conditional later identity collapses that sum to K†K. Summing a,r then gives I. Cyclicity of trace proves total output trace equals input trace. For a selected branch, normalization is nonlinear in ρ because of the dividing trace, so the selected normalized map is not itself the original linear channel. Any later resource analysis must weight branch costs and failures by their actual probabilities. This is an exact mathematical requirement, not a cosmetic reporting convention.',
'Measure a qubit in the computational basis and apply X only after outcome one.',
'Use K₀=|0⟩⟨0| and K₁=|1⟩⟨1|, with conditional U₀=I,U₁=X. The two composed Kraus operators are|0⟩⟨0| and|0⟩⟨1|. Their channel sends every density matrix to|0⟩⟨0| and is trace preserving. If only the first branch were retained, success probability would beρ₀₀; on input|1⟩ it is zero even though the selected output formula might look ideal on successful inputs.',
'The model turns branch-resolved closure into explicit probability-preserving algebra.',
'This is a finite instrument theorem, not a certificate for a Fibonacci-anyon F-symbol table, a leakage-free six-anyon encoding or a hardware threshold. Those need their actual matrices and sectors.',
'For the proposed anyonic protocol, list every allowed charge branch, prove its completeness relation on each input sector and compute both leakage probability and expected feedforward cost.',['Quantum information'])

add('Two-sector failure has a correlation parameter and a rigorous confidence envelope','two-sector failure','Probability and fault bounds','When is P_X+P_Z−P_XP_Z valid, and what can be asserted without independence?',
'The source gives a two-sector failure product and fitted threshold candidates. We replace an implicit independence assumption with an exact joint-probability formula, then add a finite confidence calculation. Threshold fitting is kept distinct from a theorem about arbitrarily large devices.',
['Let X and Z be failure events with probabilities p_X,p_Z and intersection r. Their combined failure is the union. Feasibility requires max(0,p_X+p_Z−1)≤r≤min(p_X,p_Z).','If n independent identically distributed trials each have failure probability p, the probability of zero observed failures is(1−p)^n. Independence of trials is separate from independence of sectors within one trial.'],
'P(X∪Z)=p_X+p_Z−r, hence max(p_X,p_Z)≤P(X∪Z)≤min(1,p_X+p_Z). The product formula1−(1−p_X)(1−p_Z) holds exactly when r=p_Xp_Z. After zero failures in n trials, the one-sided level1−δ upper confidence bound is p≤1−δ^{1/n}.',
'The union identity follows by partitioning into the three disjoint regions X\\Z, Z\\X and X∩Z. The intersection bounds follow from nonnegative probabilities of all four cells; conversely those bounds make the four cell probabilities r,p_X−r,p_Z−r,1−p_X−p_Z+r nonnegative and summing to one, so the bounds are sharp. Sector independence is exactly the additional equality r=p_Xp_Z. For the zero-failure bound, a candidate p above1−δ^{1/n} makes the zero-count event have probability belowδ. Inverting this binomial test gives the stated confidence procedure; it is a coverage statement about repeated sampling, not a posterior probability that the fixed parameter lies in the interval.',
'Take p_X=p_Z=1/10 and n=100 independent trials with zero observed combined failures.',
'Independent sectors give combined failure19/100. Perfectly coincident sectors give1/10, and disjoint sectors give1/5. At δ=1/20 the zero-failure confidence upper bound is1−(1/20)^{1/100}≈0.029513. A point estimate zero is therefore not a proof of zero failure probability.',
'The reported sector model can now state exactly where correlations and finite evidence enter.',
'The historical threshold candidates and scaling-fit R² are not independently reproduced. A finite confidence interval does not prove an asymptotic threshold or a uniform QRAM envelope.',
'Fit a joint sector model with an explicit covariance estimate, validate independent trial units, and derive a simultaneous finite-distance upper envelope before extrapolating in code distance or memory size.',['Probability'])

add('A weighted path majorant proves an exponential tail under an explicit row-sum bound','weighted SAW bounds','Combinatorial fault bounds','Which exact graph inequality turns a local hazard budget into a convergent path sum?',
'The June source reports weighted self-avoiding-walk quantities below one while a scalar proxy failed. A finite matrix majorant gives a genuine sufficient criterion. It also makes clear that a finite observed nth-root value is not by itself a bound for every future length.',
['Let B be a finite nonnegative transition matrix on directed edge states, chosen so that every allowed nonbacktracking fault walk is represented. Let its maximum row sum be r<1.','For a nonnegative initial row u and terminal weights v with ||v||∞≤1, the length-n majorant is Z_n=uB^nv. A self-avoiding path family is a subset of these represented walks.'],
'Z_n≤||u||₁r^n, and Σ_{n≥d}Z_n≤||u||₁r^d/(1−r). The same upper bound holds for the summed weights of self-avoiding paths. A more flexible certificate is Bw≤rw for a strictly positive vector w, using the corresponding weighted norm.',
'For nonnegative B, B1≤r1. Induction gives B^n1≤r^n1, so uB^nv≤uB^n1≤||u||₁r^n. Sum the geometric series for the tail. Dropping forbidden self-intersections only removes nonnegative path weights, proving the subset bound. For the weighted version, conjugate B by diag(w): its row sums are (Bw)_i/w_i≤r, so the same proof applies after transforming the initial and terminal weights. This is a finite Perron-style certificate and can capture heterogeneity lost in a uniform scalar degree bound. However a computed Z_12^{1/12}<1 alone does not imply an eventual r<1: a graph can have a low-weight transient region followed by a high-growth component that is reached later.',
'Take B=[[1/5,1/10],[1/4,1/4]], u=(1,0), v=(1,1).',
'The maximum row sum is1/2, so Z_n≤2^{−n}. The tail from d=4 is at most1/8. Direct multiplication gives Z₀=1,Z₁=3/10,Z₂=11/100. These actual values are smaller than the majorant but do not change its universal proof. A weighted vector can reduce the bound when a common positive subeigenvector is available.',
'The model supplies an explicit sufficient exponential envelope, not just a fitted path statistic.',
'The source’s numericalΣ_w andZ₁₂ values are not recomputed without the native graph. A fault-probability theorem additionally needs a justified mapping from physical errors to weighted paths and a union bound over all relevant starts.',
'Extract the native fault graph, exhibit a rational positive subeigenvector with r<1, and include the number of start sites to derive a complete finite-device failure bound.',['Probability','Convex optimization'])

add('The discriminant-minus-twenty-three cubic has S₃ closure and cannot lie in an abelian cyclotomic field','2026-06-15T18:52:20Z','Algebraic number theory','What exact arithmetic obstruction rules out a purely cyclotomic realization of the recovered cubic shape?',
'The knot source identifies a cubic with discriminant−23 and states that an abelian cyclotomic degeneration cannot generate it. That assertion has a precise Galois-theoretic proof. It does not establish a full state-integral or knot-triangulation comparison.',
['Let f(u)=u³−u+1. A cubic over Q is irreducible if it has no rational root. For u³+pu+q its discriminant is−4p³−27q².','A cyclotomic field Q(ζ_m) is finite Galois with abelian Galois group. Every intermediate field of an abelian Galois extension is itself Galois over Q.'],
'f is irreducible with discriminant−23, its splitting field has Galois group S₃, and no root belongs to any cyclotomic field.',
'The rational-root theorem restricts a rational root to±1, and both give f=1, so f is irreducible. The discriminant formula gives4−27=−23. The Galois group of an irreducible cubic is a transitive subgroup of S₃, hence either A₃ or S₃. The product of the three root differences changes by the sign character; its square is the discriminant. Consequently the group lies in A₃ exactly when the discriminant is a rational square. Here it is not, so the group is S₃. The single-root field has degree three and is not normal: its normal closure has degree six. If a root lay in a cyclotomic field, its degree-three subfield would be an intermediate field of an abelian Galois extension, hence normal, a contradiction. Equivalently every subfield corresponds to a normal subgroup because the ambient Galois group is abelian. This rules out even an unramified choice of a much larger cyclotomic conductor; it is a structural obstruction rather than a small-conductor search failure.',
'Locate the real root and describe the other two roots.',
'f(−4/3)=−1/27 and f(−13/10)=103/1000, so one real root lies between those rationals. Negative discriminant implies one real root and one complex-conjugate pair. Adjoining√(−23) to the real cubic field supplies its quadratic resolvent and produces the degree-six normal closure.',
'The source’s abelian-versus-S₃ barrier is a proved arithmetic theorem.',
'The specific knot interpretation, its volume and the proposed Potts bridge require independent gluing and branch data. A Galois obstruction alone does not prove that a particular analytic degeneration is impossible after arbitrary enlargement beyond abelian fields.',
'Construct the exact nonabelian field extension and express every proposed corner parameter in its basis, then compare branch embeddings and gluing equations without replacing the extension by a cyclotomic proxy.',['Galois theory'])

add('A singular corner equation is a birational form of the cubic and has a computable divisor','exact \\(5_2','Algebraic curves','How does (1−z)³=z² produce u³−u+1, and which zeros and poles belong to the corner function?',
'The recovered source gives both corner and cubic equations together with a divisor. We prove their exact relation. The calculation distinguishes the divisor of a rational function from a claimed equality of full knot, Potts or regulator theories.',
['On P¹ define R(z)=(1−z)³/z². Its finite nonzero solutions of R(z)=1 satisfy z³−2z²+3z−1=0.','A divisor of a rational function records zeros positively and poles negatively, including the point at infinity. Set u=(z−1)/z and z=1/(1−u), inverse Möbius coordinate changes away from their poles.'],
'R has divisor3[1]−2[0]−[∞]. The substitution u=(z−1)/z sends the corner equation exactly to u³−u+1=0, with no lost solution; both cubics have discriminant−23.',
'The numerator has a triple zero at z=1 and the denominator a double pole at z=0. As z→∞, R(z)~−z, so it has a simple pole at infinity, proving the divisor and its total degree zero. Use u=−(1−z)/z=(z−1)/z.  Since1−z=−uz and z=1/(1−u), the corner equation gives−u³z=1, hence u³−u+1=0. All solutions have z≠0,1 and u≠0,1, so multiplying by these denominators introduces no roots. Substitution or the general cubic discriminant formula gives−23 for z³−2z²+3z−1 as well. The substitution and its inverse verify equality of the two cubic fields without numerical root matching.',
'Take u=(z−1)/z and solve back for z.',
'z=1/(1−u), and f(u)=0 implies1−u=−u³. Thus z=−u^{−3}, an exact field identity. Conversely z³−2z²+3z−1=0 reduces f((z−1)/z) to zero after multiplication by z³. The transformation preserves the degree-three field and each chosen complex embedding.',
'The corner and cubic fields are related by a verified rational map.',
'The source cubic equivalence needs the correct substitution, which is u=(z−1)/z; A divisor identity does not compute a dilogarithm volume.',
'For the actual multi-edge gluing system, derive every shape substitution and logarithmic flattening from its equations, then verify branch compatibility and the claimed regulator combination.',['Galois theory','Dilogarithm'])

add('Bloch–Wigner values give a convergent regular-shape computation with an explicit tail','DT/DU','Dilogarithms','What can be computed rigorously from a declared tetrahedral shape before asserting a knot-volume identity?',
'The source names the figure-eight expression twice the imaginary dilogarithm and a separate5₂ volume target. We develop the regular-shape special value exactly and bound its series tail. The geometric identification with an ideal tetrahedron is classical input; the triangulation of a particular knot must still be supplied.',
['Define D(z)=ImLi₂(z)+log|z|arg(1−z), with the usual Bloch–Wigner branch-independent continuation. On |z|=1,z≠1, D(e^{iθ})=Σ_{n≥1}sin(nθ)/n².','For ζ=e^{iπ/3}, |ζ|=1, so the logarithmic correction is zero. An oriented ideal tetrahedron of shape ζ is regular; its volume equals D(ζ), a classical hyperbolic-volume theorem.'],
'D(ζ)=Cl₂(π/3)>0, and the N-term series has absolute tail at most1/N. Therefore the two-regular-tetrahedra volume2D(ζ) is about2.0298832128, with a rigorously controlled series error. It is different from the reported5₂ target2.8281220883.',
'The dilogarithm power series Σz^n/n² converges absolutely on the unit circle, so its imaginary part is exactly the sine series. The omitted absolute sum is at mostΣ_{n>N}1/n²≤∫_N∞x^{−2}dx=1/N. Positivity can be seen from the integral identity Cl₂(θ)=−∫₀^θlog(2sin(t/2))dt: on0<t<π/3,2sin(t/2)<1, making the integrand positive. The derivative identity follows by Abel damping the Fourier series of−log(2sin(t/2)), integrating, and letting the damping tend to one; the logarithmic endpoint is integrable. A complete two-tetrahedron triangulation with compatible positive orientations then adds the two volumes by the classical volume theorem. The numerical value alone cannot identify which manifold a shape belongs to.',
'Compute the series at ζ using its period-six sine pattern.',
'The sine values repeat√3/2,√3/2,0,−√3/2,−√3/2,0. Thus the series reduces to a rapidly checkable alternating block sum over residue classes mod6. Keeping100000 terms bounds the error in2D by2×10^{−5}, independently of floating arithmetic error. Grouped-block cancellation gives a stronger tail if desired.',
'The treatment supplies an actual special-function calculation and a declared geometric input.',
'It does not certify the source5₂ benchmark or any state-integral asymptotic. Those need the correct shapes, flattenings, contour and normalization; a nearby numerical value is insufficient.',
'For the recovered cubic complex root, derive a branch-safe dilogarithm combination and interval-enclose it, then compare it with a separately certified knot triangulation volume.',['Dilogarithm','Complex analysis'])

add('Branch periods detect monodromy that a local curl test misses','exact branch periods','Complex analysis and monodromy','Why can a locally exact differential have a nonzero closed-loop period?',
'The June source demotes a wavelet curl-flux heuristic and retains exact branch periods as a viable obstruction. The logarithm on the punctured plane is a constructive example: every local derivative is regular, but a loop around the missing point detects the obstruction to one global branch.',
['Let U=C\\{0} and η=dz/z. A local branch of logz has differential η. A closed piecewise smooth loop γ in U has integer winding number wind(γ,0).','A globally exact one-form is dF for a single-valued differentiable function F. Such a form has zero integral around every closed loop.'],
'∮_γη=2πi wind(γ,0). In particular η is closed but not globally exact on U. Analytic continuation of a logarithm around γ changes its value by that period.',
'For the unit circle γ(t)=e^{it},0≤t≤2π, the integrand is i dt and the period is2πi. More generally the map γ/|γ| to the circle has a continuous argument lift along the interval parameterizing the loop; its endpoint change is2πm for an integer m. Writing locally logγ=log|γ|+iargγ and integrating its derivative yields2πim, which is the winding number formula. Closedness follows because η is holomorphic on U, or directly by differentiating in real coordinates. If η=dF globally, the fundamental theorem along a closed loop would give zero, contradicting the unit-circle calculation. The failure is global topology, not a local nonzero curl. In a simply connected slit domain avoiding zero, a branch exists and every loop period vanishes, consistently with the theorem.',
'Use a circle γ_R(t)=Re^{it} and a twice-wound version.',
'The first integral is2πi for everyR>0; the radius cancels. The twice-wound loop gives4πi. A small contractible loop centered away from zero gives zero. Therefore shrinking a loop without crossing the singularity does not remove its period, whereas a pointwise local curl statistic is zero everywhere on U.',
'The source’s retained obstruction has a rigorous invariant and an explicit computation.',
'A period of an actual multi-valued state-integral phase needs that phase’s differential and branch singularities. This example does not validate an unrecovered wavelet-derived obstruction or identify every possible monodromy.',
'For a specified algebraic curve and logarithmic phase, choose a homology basis of cycles, compute all exact residue periods and track their integer monodromy under parameter continuation.',['Complex analysis'])

add('A cubic fold produces the three-halves action law before any thimble claim','Level 4 closed','Asymptotic analysis','Which local normal form proves a3/2 saddle-action exponent, and what extra input determines contour coefficients?',
'The genuine June18 assistant fragment records a geometric saddle intersection claim. We develop a newly stipulated cubic-fold model to explain a possible local three-halves exponent, without attributing an Airy theorem or verified contour calculation to that fragment. The local phase does not determine global integration-cycle coefficients.',
['Let Φ(x;t)=x³/3−tx with real t>0. Its critical points are x_±=±√t. Consider a Laplace-type integrand e^{−Φ/h} on a contour for which it converges.','A thimble coefficient is an integer determined by a chosen contour’s intersections with dual steepest-ascent cycles. It is not determined solely by the list of critical points.'],
'Φ(x_−;t)−Φ(x_+;t)=4t^{3/2}/3. Under x=h^{1/3}y the exponent becomes y³/3−(t/h^{2/3})y, so the fold transition scale is t≈h^{2/3}. The Hessians at the saddles have magnitude2√t and degenerate at t=0.',
'The critical equation x²−t=0 gives the two roots. Substitution yields Φ(√t;t)=−2t^{3/2}/3 and Φ(−√t;t)=2t^{3/2}/3, proving the action difference. The second derivative is2x, giving opposite Hessian signs and magnitude2√t. Rescaling x by h^{1/3} removes h from the cubic term and leaves the single scaled parameter t/h^{2/3}; therefore a Gaussian approximation at two separated saddles becomes nonuniform as that parameter approaches zero. This is the precise mechanism behind an Airy uniform approximation. Yet choosing the real line is not automatically permissible for e^{−x³/(3h)} because one end diverges. A convergent contour must run into sectors where the cubic real part is positive, and different contour combinations can have different saddle coefficients while sharing the same local phase.',
'Take t=1/4.',
'The saddles are±1/2, their phase values are∓1/12 and their difference is1/6, agreeing with(4/3)(1/4)^{3/2}. If t is multiplied by four the action gap is multiplied by eight. At h=1/1000 the transition scale h^{2/3}=1/100, so t=1/4 lies well outside the coalescence scale in this stipulated model.',
'The three-halves exponent has an exact local derivation and a clear contour limitation.',
'This does not prove any historical exponent fit, geometric saddle coefficient or full two-dimensional contour problem. A local fold cannot determine global Stokes data.',
'For the actual state-integral phase, prove a nondegenerate transverse reduction to this normal form, construct convergent contour ends and compute the full relative-homology intersection pairing.',['Airy functions'])

add('A lattice parallelotope tiles space and basis changes preserve covolume','fundamental parallelotope','Geometry of numbers','Which exact lattice invariants survive a basis change, and how does a half-open cell avoid overlap?',
'The June lattice material gives a fundamental parallelotope, covolume and successive minima. We prove the tiling and basis invariance explicitly and compute a nonorthogonal example. The result provides genuine geometry without implying a compiler speedup.',
['Let B be an invertible real n-by-n matrix, L=BZ^n and P=B[0,1)^n. Its covolume is Lebesgue volume of P.','A unimodular integer matrix U has determinant±1 and inverse integral. Successive minima λ_i(L) are the least radii of Euclidean balls containing i linearly independent lattice vectors.'],
'The translates P+ℓ,ℓ∈L, partition R^n uniquely. VolP=|detB|. Two bases B and BU with U unimodular generate the same lattice and have the same covolume; their successive minima also coincide because these depend on the lattice set.',
'For any x, write y=B^{−1}x and decompose each coordinate uniquely as its integer floor plus a fractional part in[0,1). This gives x=Bk+Bf with k∈Z^n and f∈[0,1)^n. Uniqueness of integer and fractional parts gives a genuine partition, including boundary points under the half-open convention. Linear change of variables multiplies volume by |detB|. If U is unimodular, UZ^n=Z^n, so BU generates the same set. Conversely if B and B′ generate the same lattice, U=B^{−1}B′ and U^{−1} have integer entries, forcing detU=±1. The successive-minimum statement then follows from equality of the lattice sets, not from a false claim that a nonorthogonal basis preserves the lengths of individual coefficient vectors.',
'Take B=[[2,1],[0,1]].',
'The covolume is two. L consists of integer vectors(x,y) with x−y even. The shortest nonzero vectors are(1,1),(1,−1) and their negatives, all of length√2; the first two are independent, so λ₁=λ₂=√2. Multiplying B by U=[[1,1],[0,1]] gives a visually different skew cell but the same lattice and area. The displayed original basis vector(2,0) is longer than λ₁, illustrating why a basis column need not be a shortest vector.',
'The lattice model has exact tiling, determinant and shortest-vector certificates.',
'No determinant ratio alone proves a runtime or arithmetic-operation reduction. An application must specify which lattice and which algorithm use the basis.',
'For a conductor lattice, derive its exact integer basis and use a proved reduction algorithm to compare basis conditioning, enumeration cost and invariant covolume separately.',['Lattices'])

add('Planar zonotope area is a determinant sum, not a standalone runtime ratio','zonotope geometry','Convex geometry','Which determinant-mass identity can be proved exactly, and what information is lost by collapsing it to a scalar?',
'The source connects zonotope determinant mass to conductor compilation. We give a complete planar theorem and a worked exact example. It demonstrates useful geometry while keeping the proposed speedup ratio distinct from measured or proved algorithmic complexity.',
['For vectors v₁,…,v_m∈R², define Z=Σ_i[0,v_i], the Minkowski sum of segments. Parallel generators are allowed.','For a convex planar body K, adding a segment [0,v] increases area by |v| times the width of K perpendicular to v. A segment has zero area but contributes width.'],
'AreaZ=Σ_{i<j}|det(v_i,v_j)|. In particular equal area does not force equal numbers of generators, identical dependence patterns or identical implementation cost.',
'Rotate coordinates so v is horizontal. Every horizontal slice of a convex body is an interval, and adding [0,v] increases each nonempty slice length by |v|. Integrating over the perpendicular coordinate increases area by |v| times the perpendicular projection length. For a zonotope generated by earlier segments, that projection is the interval sum of their projections, so its length is the sum of their absolute perpendicular projection lengths. Multiplication by |v| turns each into |det(v_i,v)|. Starting from one zero-area segment and adding generators proves the determinant formula by induction. This is an exact proof even with collinear vectors, whose pair determinants vanish. The higher-dimensional determinant-sum theorem is classical, but the planar result already suffices for the worked certificate here.',
'Use generators v₁=(1,0),v₂=(0,1),v₃=(1,1).',
'The three absolute determinants are1,1,1, giving area three. The convex hull is the hexagon(0,0),(1,0),(2,1),(2,2),(1,2),(0,1), whose shoelace area is also three. Replacing v₁ by two collinear half-segments leaves Z unchanged and leaves the determinant sum three, while the generator list length increases from three to four. Therefore area does not by itself determine a count of controlled operations.',
'The determinant mass has a rigorous geometric meaning and an exact polygon cross-check.',
'The source’s2.5–5× modeled speedups are not established by this theorem. Its actual weighted projected determinant mass needs the specified projection, weights and ambient rank.',
'Derive the higher-dimensional weighted formula for the actual projected conductor vectors, identify zero minors exactly and compare operation counts under a fixed implementation cost model.',['Lattices','Convex optimization'])

add('The quartic discriminant−283 is exact, but its claimed global uniqueness is not established','For \\(K=','Algebraic number theory','Which properties of the recovered quartic can be proved directly without accepting a probabilistic uniqueness argument?',
'The external June file asserts a quartic uniqueness theorem involving enumeration, a probabilistic middle range and large bounds. We do not promote that claim. Instead we prove the concrete quartic’s irreducibility, discriminant and signature and construct explicit exceptional units.',
['Let f(x)=x⁴−x−1 and K=Q(α) for a root α. Its polynomial discriminant is the resultant Res(f,f′), since the quartic sign factor is positive.','An exceptional unit is an algebraic integer z for which both z and1−z are units. The field signature(r₁,r₂) records real embeddings and conjugate pairs of complex embeddings.'],
'f is irreducible over Q, has discriminant−283 and signature(2,1). Both α and1−α are units, so α is an exceptional unit. The polynomial order Z[α] is maximal because its squarefree discriminant leaves no nontrivial square index.',
'Modulo two the polynomial is x⁴+x+1. It has no root in F₂. The only monic irreducible quadratic is x²+x+1; reducing x⁴+x+1 modulo that polynomial gives one, so there is no quadratic factor. Hence it is irreducible over F₂ and therefore over Q by Gauss’s lemma. The depressed-quartic formula for x⁴+bx+c gives discriminant256c³−27b⁴; substituting b=c=−1 yields−256−27=−283. This can also be checked as the determinant of the7-by7 Sylvester matrix, which the verifier does exactly. The derivative4x³−1 has one real root, so f decreases then increases and has at most two real zeros. Since f(−1)=1,f(0)=−1,f(2)=13, there are two, leaving one complex pair. Normα=f(0)=−1 and Norm(1−α)=f(1)=−1, proving both are units. Finally discZ[α]=[O_K:Z[α]]²discK; squarefreeness forces index one.',
'Produce unit identities without numerical root approximations.',
'From α⁴−α=1 we get α(α³−1)=1, so α^{−1}=α³−1. Also f(1)=−1 implies1−α has norm−1 and an integral inverse obtainable by polynomial division. All four embeddings have the same integral norm identities, although their absolute values differ.',
'The actual field has strong exact arithmetic structure without a fabricated global classification.',
'No uniqueness among all corner fields or quartics is proved. A probabilistic exclusion of an infinite parameter range is not an exhaustive deterministic theorem.',
'State a bounded search domain for corner exponents and prove a complete exact enumeration there; any global extension needs independently verified effective bounds covering every omitted case.',['Number fields'])

add('Corner pins generate exceptional units and a finite anharmonic orbit','corner pins satisfy','Diophantine equations','What exact unit consequence follows from a signed corner equation, and how do its six cross-ratio transforms behave?',
'The external research program proposes corner equations and several variants. We give a useful elementary arithmetic theorem valid for positive exponents. It yields a finite orbit of exceptional units but avoids assuming that six written formulas produce six distinct field elements.',
['Let z be an algebraic integer and suppose (1−z)^a=εz^b, where a,b are positive integers and ε is an algebraic unit.','The anharmonic transforms are z,1−z,1/z,1/(1−z),z/(z−1),(z−1)/z, defined for z≠0,1.'],
'z and1−z are units. Every anharmonic transform is also an exceptional unit and lies in the same number field. The orbit has at most six elements and can be smaller at special values.',
'The principal ideals(z) and(1−z) are coprime because their sum contains z+(1−z)=1. The corner equation says(1−z)^a=(z)^b as ideals, since ε is a unit. If a nonzero prime ideal divided(z), it would divide the right side and hence(1−z), contradicting coprimality. Thus(z) is the unit ideal, and similarly for1−z. This proves both are units. Their inverses and quotients are therefore algebraic integers and units. For each anharmonic transform w, its complement1−w is again a signed quotient or inverse of z and1−z; for example1−1/z=(z−1)/z and1−z/(z−1)=−1/(z−1), so it too is a unit. The transformations are generated by z↦1−z and z↦1/z; their elementary composition table has at most six forms, all rationally invertible, proving the same-field and orbit statements. The argument requires positive exponents and integrality, not merely a complex numerical solution.',
'Take the golden-ratio unit z=φ satisfying(1−z)²=z^{−2}.',
'This written relation uses a negative exponent, but multiplication gives z²(1−z)²=1, directly proving both factors are units. Its six transforms reduce to φ,1−φ,φ^{−1},−φ,φ²,φ^{−2}, with possible equality checked algebraically. By contrast z=1/2 has an anharmonic orbit of only three values and is not an algebraic integer unit, showing why the unit hypotheses matter.',
'The corner program gains a rigorous bridge to exceptional-unit arithmetic and an exact finite symmetry.',
'If exponents are negative, denominators must be cleared and the unit argument rechecked. The theorem does not classify all exceptional units or prove the claimed uniqueness of discriminants−23,−31 or−283.',
'For each bounded exponent pair, compute minimal polynomials and the anharmonic orbit exactly, then apply effective S-unit bounds only with explicitly proved constants and complete coverage.',['Number fields'])

add('The Moyal bracket has a finite falling-factorial formula with even powers of ℏ','normalized Moyal bracket','Deformation quantization','Which exact polynomial coefficients detect equality of Moyal and Poisson brackets under a declared Weyl convention?',
'The June29 file gives falling-factorial coefficients and defect examples. Its displayed ℏ exponent is inconsistent with those examples. We fix the standard Weyl convention and derive the finite formula, so equality is a polynomial coefficient question rather than a numerical tolerance.',
['Define f⋆g=f exp[(iℏ/2)(←∂_q→∂_p−←∂_p→∂_q)]g and [f,g]_M=(f⋆g−g⋆f)/(iℏ). All expressions are formal polynomials for polynomial inputs. Put κ=min(a,d)+min(b,c) for the monomial formula below.','For f=q^ap^b,g=q^cp^d, let S_k=Σ_{r=0}^k(−1)^r binom(k,r)(a)_{k−r}(b)_r(c)_r(d)_{k−r}, with falling factorials zero beyond nonnegative arguments.'],
'[f,g]_M=Σ_{1≤k≤κ, k odd} (−1)^{(k−1)/2}ℏ^{k−1}S_k q^{a+c−k}p^{b+d−k}/(2^{k−1}k!). The k=1 term is the Poisson bracket (ad−bc)q^{a+c−1}p^{b+d−1}. Equality holds exactly when every active odd S_k for k≥3 vanishes.',
'Expand the exponential and use the binomial theorem on the commuting left-right derivative operators. A term choosing r copies of−∂_p on the first input and∂_q on the second gives exactly the falling-factorial product and sign in S_k. Swapping inputs changes the order-k bidifferential by(−1)^k, so the commutator cancels every even k and doubles odd k. Dividing2(iℏ/2)^k by iℏ yields the stated coefficient, including ℏ^{k−1}; a source power ℏ^{(k−1)/2} would be a different parameter convention and would not match its own ℏ² examples. Derivative orders vanish beyond total input degree, making the sum finite. Distinct odd k have distinct formal powers ofℏ, so equality to the Poisson bracket forces each coefficient separately to vanish.',
'Compute the bracket of q³ and p³.',
'S₁=9 and S₃=(3)₃²=36. Thus[q³,p³]_M=9q²p²−(36/24)ℏ²=9q²p²−3ℏ²/2. For q⁵,p⁵, S₃=3600 and S₅=14400, giving25q⁴p⁴−150ℏ²q²p²+15ℏ⁴/2.',
'The source defect examples agree with the corrected convention and supply exact finite certificates.',
'The source file is external context T, not a recovered literal user prompt. The theorem classifies equality for a given monomial pair, not every possible polynomial cancellation after summing many pairs.',
'Implement exact polynomial star multiplication and use coefficient equality to certify selected polynomial subspaces, keeping a formal-parameter theorem distinct from a fixed-numerical-ℏ identity.',['Moyal'])

add('The proposed cubic-cancellation counterexample is actually an infinite exact Moyal family','Example:','Polynomial bracket exactness','Does the recovered family have a fifth-order defect, or does degree force exactness after its cubic coefficient cancels?',
'The external June file asserts that(qp³,q^{3r+2}p^r) has S₃=0 but a nonzero S₅. The first polynomial has total degree four, so that claim cannot hold. A direct calculation proves something stronger and constructive: the entire family has exactly its Poisson bracket.',
['Use the Weyl/Moyal convention and S_k coefficients from the preceding entry. Let r be a positive integer, f=qp³ and g=q^{3r+2}p^r.','For an order-k bidifferential, exactly k derivatives act on each input. It vanishes if k exceeds either input’s total polynomial degree.'],
'For every r≥1, [qp³,q^{3r+2}p^r]_M={qp³,q^{3r+2}p^r}=−(8r+6)q^{3r+2}p^{r+2}. In particular S₅=0, contradicting the retrieved claimed nonzero fifth-order expression.',
'The first input has degree four, so every k≥5 term is identically zero. Only the possible k=3 correction remains. For a=1,b=3,c=3r+2,d=r, the terms in S₃ with r-index zero or one vanish because(a)₃=(a)₂=0. The two remaining terms give S₃=3·(1)₁(3)₂(c)₂r−(3)₃(c)₃=18c(c−1)r−6c(c−1)(c−2)=6c(c−1)[3r−(c−2)]=0. Therefore every higher odd coefficient vanishes. The Poisson coefficient is ad−bc=r−3(3r+2)=−8r−6, and the resulting exponents are a+c−1=c and b+d−1=r+2, proving the formula. This argument is uniform in r, so a finite search is corroboration rather than its proof.',
'Take r=1, giving f=qp³ and g=q⁵p.',
'The classical bracket is−14q⁵p³. The cubic coefficient is6·5·4(3−3)=0. There is no fifth-order term because no fifth derivative of qp³ can survive. The claimed source expression at r=1 would be720·1·2·4·5=28800, a direct contradiction to the derivative degree bound. The exact verifier checks the coefficient formula for many r and all active orders.',
'A false negative example is repaired into an exact infinite family with a short proof.',
'Other monomial pairs of higher total degree can have S₃=0 but nonzero S₅. This result does not assert that cubic cancellation is always sufficient; it identifies why it is sufficient in this family.',
'Classify all pairs with first monomial qp^b by factoring its odd S_k coefficients, then identify the first genuine higher-degree family where S₃ vanishes while S₅ survives.',['Moyal'])

add('A genuine fifth-order Moyal obstruction appears in a higher-degree family','higher-order cubic cancellations','Polynomial bracket exactness','Can one supply a valid exact example where the cubic correction vanishes but the fifth-order correction does not?',
'The preceding source counterexample failed its degree bound. We now build a valid replacement as new mathematics, explicitly distinguished from the historical family. It demonstrates the intended phenomenon with exact integer coefficients and no parameter ambiguity.',
['For f=qp^b and g=q^cp^d, the odd falling-factorial coefficient is S_k=(b)_{k−1}(c)_{k−1}[kd−(b−k+1)(c−k+1)].','Choose b=c=5 and d=3. Then f=qp⁵ has total degree six and g=q⁵p³ has degree eight, so k=3 and5 can both be active.'],
'For this pair S₃=0 but S₅=201600. Its Moyal bracket is−22q⁵p⁷+105ℏ⁴qp³. Hence the Poisson and Moyal brackets differ despite exact cancellation of the cubic correction.',
'Because a=1, in the sum for S_k only r=k−1 and r=k can survive. For odd k their signs are positive and negative respectively. Factoring the common(b)_{k−1}(c)_{k−1} gives the stated formula. At k=3 its bracket is3·3−(5−2)(5−2)=9−9=0. At k=5 the falling-factorial prefactor is(5)₄²=120²=14400 and the bracket is15−1=14, giving S₅=201600 and Moyal coefficient201600/(2⁴·5!)=105. With the stipulated exponents the Poisson coefficient is1·3−5·5=−22 and the k=5 output exponents are1+5−5=1 and5+3−5=3. Every odd k≥7 vanishes because the first input has total degree six. Therefore the corrected exact bracket is−22q⁵p⁷+105ℏ⁴qp³. All coefficients are integers before the declared factorial normalization, so this is an exact algebraic certificate.',
'Evaluate at q=p=ℏ=1.',
'The Poisson value is−22 and the exact Moyal value is83. The difference105 is entirely fifth order; the ℏ² coefficient is zero. This is the intended higher-order obstruction with sufficient total degree to support it. An independent direct star-product expansion gives the same coefficient.',
'The example proves that vanishing S₃ alone is insufficient once fifth derivatives can survive.',
'This is a newly stipulated replacement example. It must not be attributed to the June source, whose written family was different. No complete high-degree classification is claimed.',
'Use the factored formula to solve the Diophantine cubic-cancellation equation3d=(b−2)(c−2), then characterize which integer solutions also annihilate S₅ and subsequent active odd terms.',['Moyal'])

add('Breath-stream capture arithmetic is a transparent volume-to-mass balance','150 m³/h','Applied dimensional mathematics','Which units and averaging interval produce a stated carbon-dioxide mass from a dilute gas stream?',
'The June applied discussion supplies gas fractions, volumetric flow, efficiency and duration. We extract a generic dimensional balance only, avoiding health, process or business recommendations. Every numerical value below is a stipulated model input rather than a new measurement.',
['Let Q be a gas volume flow in m³/hour, x its carbon-dioxide volume fraction, η∈[0,1] a captured fraction and t elapsed hours. At fixed reference conditions let ρ be carbon-dioxide density in kg/m³.','For time-varying data, captured mass is m=∫Q(t)x(t)η(t)ρ(t)dt. The constant-input formula is m=Qxηρt. Parts per million means x=ppm×10^{−6}.'],
'The model predicts11.664 kg forQ=150,x=0.003,η=0.6,ρ=1.8,t=24. ForQ=120,x=0.006 andt=2 it predicts1.5552 kg. Multiplying separate averages of Q,x andη is not generally equal to averaging their product.',
'The flow of carbon-dioxide volume isQx, measured in m³/hour. Multiplying by density gives kg/hour, multiplying by recoveryη gives the captured rate and integrating over hours gives kilograms. The ppm conversion is essential:3000ppm is0.003, not0.3. For varying inputs the integral follows by summing small time intervals and taking the Riemann limit under ordinary bounded regularity. The product-of-averages issue is already visible with two time intervals: if high flow always coincides with low concentration, the covariance is negative and EQx<EQ Ex. A concentration average must be flow-weighted to preserve the total mass, namely x_weighted=∫Qx/∫Q when the denominator is positive. Efficiency and density variations require their own weighting as well.',
'Compare two equal one-hour periods with(Q,x)=(100,0.01) and(200,0), withη=1 andρ=1.8.',
'The exact captured mass is1.8kg. Using separate averages givesQ̄=150 andx̄=0.005, predicting2.7kg over two hours, an overestimate of50%. The flow-weighted concentration is1/300, which restores the correct result. This error is purely mathematical and does not require any claim about an actual biological source.',
'The source throughput arithmetic is recoverable and its averaging assumptions are explicit.',
'Gas density depends on reference pressure and temperature. The formula does not certify practical capture, compression, sorbent capacity, dry-ice production or source safety.',
'Propagate interval-valued sensor uncertainty through the time integral and derive a conservative mass bound using measured flow-concentration covariance rather than multiplying independent nominal averages.',['Convex optimization'])

add('The SU₂ sine transform proves orthogonality and its global dimension formula','CO finite','Finite modular data','Which exact trigonometric identities justify the reported conductor formula D²=L/[2sin²(π/L)]?',
'The June source lists finite modular and Weyl data with conductorL. We prove the sine-matrix identities directly. This is a well-defined finite algebraic calculation; a full modular tensor category additionally requires its actual fusion and coherence data.',
['Fix integerL≥3 and labelsr,s=1,…,L−1. Define S_rs=√(2/L)sin(πrs/L). The distinguished label isr=1.','Set d_r=S_r1/S_11=sin(πr/L)/sin(π/L). These are the standard positive sine dimensions for the SU₂ levelk=L−2 convention.'],
'S is real orthogonal, and Σ_{r=1}^{L−1}d_r²=L/[2sin²(π/L)]. All d_r are positive in the distinguished real embedding. The normalization containsL=k+2, notk.',
'Use2sinA sinB=cos(A−B)−cos(A+B). Summing over the finite half-grid, or equivalently taking the odd part of the Fourier transform on Z/(2L), givesΣ_{s=1}^{L−1}sin(πrs/L)sin(πts/L)=(L/2)δ_rt. Multiplication by2/L proves orthogonality. Takingr=t=1 in this identity givesΣ_ssin²(πs/L)=L/2; dividing bysin²(π/L) proves the global-dimension formula. Every angleπr/L lies strictly between zero andπ, so each sine dimension is positive. If a Galois automorphism changes the root of unity, that positivity need not persist in its new embedding; it is an additional arithmetic criterion rather than a consequence of the abstract conductor alone.',
'TakeL=5, corresponding tok=3.',
'The dimension list is1,φ,φ,1 because sin(2π/5)/sin(π/5)=φ. The squared global dimension is2+2φ²=5+√5, agreeing with5/[2sin²(π/5)]. AtL=3 the list is1,1 and the global dimension is two. These examples fix the denominator and the level shift exactly.',
'The source conductor expression has a complete normalized finite proof.',
'No universal categorical repair, arbitrary higher-rank Galois positivity result or pentagon solution follows from this sine matrix. The source’s unresolved spinor F-blocks remain unresolved.',
'For a specified level and Galois embedding, determine the sign pattern of every sine dimension and verify the chosen fusion coefficients, then separate positivity of this vector from existence of a unitary associator.',['SU2 modular data','Weil'])

add('A torus mirror cocycle can carry exactly one binary obstruction','intrinsic translations','Group cohomology','Which concrete action produces the source Z/2 mirror class, and why is the module part of the theorem?',
'The followup reports a wallpaper-related mirrorH¹=Z/2. That statement requires an action and coefficient module. We give an exact rank-one sign-action calculation and a contrasting trivial-action case, so the binary obstruction is a theorem with explicit inputs.',
['LetC₂={1,s} act onM=Z bys·m=−m. A1-cocycle is determined byx=f(s) withf(s²)=x+s·x=0. A1-coboundary hasf(s)=s·m−m.','For comparison, let the same group act trivially onZ. Cohomology uses the same definitions but a different action.'],
'For the sign action,H¹(C₂,Z_sign)≅Z/2Z. For the trivial action,H¹(C₂,Z)=0. Therefore an asserted mirror class cannot be read independently of its translation coefficient action.',
'Under the sign action the cocycle condition isx−x=0, so every integerx is a cocycle. Coboundaries are−2m, exactly the even integers. Quotienting givesZ/2, with parity the complete invariant. Under the trivial action the condition is2x=0, forcingx=0 becauseZ has no2-torsion; the cohomology is therefore zero. In a geometric extension picture, changing a lift by an integral translation changes the sign-action cocycle by an even integer, so odd and even lifts cannot be identified by that adjustment. For torus-valued coefficientsM=R/Z the answer changes again: multiplication bytwo is surjective, making the corresponding quotient vanish. This reinforces that one must state whether translations are integral, real or torus-valued.',
'Use cocyclesx=1andx=3for the sign action.',
'They differ bytwo and represent the same nonzero class. The cocyclex=2is a coboundary, obtained fromm=−1, whilex=1is not a coboundary because no integerm satisfies−2m=1. Passing toR/Z permitsm=−1/2and removes this particular obstruction.',
'The binary class is computed exactly, including its change under coefficients.',
'This calculation does not classify all wallpaper extensions or verify the source’s primitive nonsymmorphic list. A full2D lattice action needs its actual matrices and the appropriate extension-degree cohomology.',
'For each specified mirror or dihedral lattice action, compute the integral cochain maps and Smith forms, and identify precisely whetherH¹, H²or a quotient of translations classifies the proposed geometric data.',['Group cohomology'])

add('The totient Dirichlet series has the source zeta majorant on its correct half-plane','majorant','Analytic number theory','Why does Σφ(n)n^{−σ} equalζ(σ−1)/ζ(σ), and where is absolute convergence required?',
'The June finite-module followup supplies the majorantζ(σ−1)/ζ(σ)−1 forσ>2. We prove the arithmetic series exactly and give a finite tail bound. This is a valid analytic input for a defect bound only when that defect is actually dominated by the stated coefficients.',
['Euler’s totientφ(n) counts units modulo n. For realσ>2 defineF(σ)=Σ_{n≥1}φ(n)n^{−σ}. Letμ be the Möbius function andid(n)=n.','The convolution identityφ=μ*id follows fromΣ_{d|n}φ(d)=n. Absolute convergence permits multiplication and rearrangement of the Dirichlet series.'],
'F(σ)=ζ(σ−1)/ζ(σ) forσ>2, soΣ_{n≥2}φ(n)n^{−σ}=ζ(σ−1)/ζ(σ)−1. The tail afterN is at mostN^{2−σ}/(σ−2).',
'Count the integers1,…,n by theirgcdwithn: those withn/gcd equal tod are counted byφ(d), provingΣ_{d|n}φ(d)=n. Möbius inversion givesφ(n)=Σ_{d|n}μ(d)n/d. Sinceφ(n)≤n, its series converges absolutely forσ>2. Expanding the convolution and changingn=dm yieldsΣ_dμ(d)d^{−σ}Σ_mm^{1−σ}. The second factor isζ(σ−1). The first is1/ζ(σ) by the absolutely convergent Euler product forσ>1. This proves the identity. For the tail,φ(n)n^{−σ}≤n^{1−σ}, a decreasing positive function, soΣ_{n>N}n^{1−σ}≤∫_N∞x^{1−σ}dx=N^{2−σ}/(σ−2). No continuation beyond the absolute-convergence region is needed or used in this positive majorant.',
'Atσ=3keep the termsn≤10.',
'The partial sum is rational and lies belowζ(2)/ζ(3). Its omitted tail is at most1/10. Excludingn=1subtracts exactlyone. A defect sequence bounded in absolute value byφ(n)n^{−3} therefore has an absolutely convergent total bounded byζ(2)/ζ(3)−1, provided the coefficientwise inequality is proved.',
'The source majorant has a precise identity, convergence domain and explicit numerical truncation control.',
'The theorem does not prove an unspecified twisted-zeta defect satisfies the coefficientwise bound, nor does it establish an Euler-product weld at roots of unity.',
'For the actual cocycle defect, derive its local coefficient bound and exploit cancellation only after absolute convergence or a justified summation procedure has been established.',['Number fields'])

add('The recovered Habiro matrix has a free direction that must be removed before Gaussian integration','exact \\(5_2','Degenerate quadratic forms','How does an exact kernel change a Gaussian saddle calculation and its regularization?',
'The source supplies a three-by-three matrix and kernel. We derive its full spectrum and the precise divergent Gaussian factor. This is constructive saddle mathematics while keeping the arithmetic knot interpretation and any contour selection separate.',
['LetA=[[2,1,1],[1,1,0],[1,0,1]]. Its quadratic form isQ(x)=xᵀAx. LetK=span((-1,1,1)) andV=K⊥ with its Euclidean area measure.','Forε>0 define the regularized integralI_ε=∫_{R³}exp[−xᵀ(A+εI)x/2]dx. The ordinary real Gaussian formula applies only to a positive-definite matrix.'],
'A is positive semidefinite with eigenvalues0,1,3 and kernelK. The unrestricted Gaussian withε=0 diverges. The integral restricted toV is2π/√3, and√(ε/(2π))I_ε tends to that value asε↓0.',
'ExpandQ=(x₁+x₂)²+(x₁+x₃)². It vanishes exactly whenx₂=x₃=−x₁, proving the kernel and positivity. The vectors(0,1,−1) and(2,1,1) are orthogonal eigenvectors with eigenvaluesone andthree. Together with the kernel vector they give an orthogonal eigenbasis. In orthonormal coordinates(u₀,u₁,u₃),Q=u₁²+3u₃² and theu₀integral is∫_R1du₀=∞. Removing that direction gives two independent Gaussian integrals,√(2π)√(2π/3)=2π/√3. Regularization shifts the eigenvalues toε,1+ε,3+ε, soI_ε=(2π)^{3/2}/√[ε(1+ε)(3+ε)]. Multiplying by√(ε/(2π)) isolates the finite transverse factor and proves the limit. A determinant-zero matrix cannot be used in the nondegenerate three-dimensional stationary-phase formula without this reduction.',
'Atε=1the eigenvalues are1,2,4.',
'I₁=(2π)^{3/2}/√8. Asε→0the leading divergence is√(2π/ε)times2π/√3. The pseudodeterminant, product of nonzero eigenvalues, isthree, whereasdetA=0; confusing these two would erase the free direction.',
'The source kernel has a complete spectral and integral interpretation.',
'The Euclidean real contour here is newly stipulated. An oscillatory state integral may require a different contour, phase convention and treatment of a gauge volume.',
'For the actual nonlinear phase, quotient the exact symmetry or integrate its free variable with a specified measure, then prove the transverse Hessian controls the reduced saddle uniformly.',['Convex optimization'])

add('Alternating projections have an exact contraction factor and a distinct commutator norm','2026-06-25T20:02:36Z','Linear closure dynamics','Which two-line model proves the source formulasρ=cos²θ and||[P,Q]||=|sin2θ|/2?',
'The late-June closure calculus includes projection commutators and a convergence table. A two-dimensional model proves both constants exactly and shows that commutation defect and iterative contraction measure different features.',
['LetPproject orthogonally ontoe₁inR² andQonto v=(cosθ,sinθ), with0<θ<π/2. Setc=cosθ,s=sinθ.','One cycle acting on thePline isPQP. The commutator is[P,Q]=PQ−QP, measured in Euclidean operator norm.'],
'OnimP, the cyclePQPcontracts byρ=c². Itsn-th iterate isρ^nP. The commutator norm is|cs|=|sin2θ|/2. To reachρ^n≤εwith0<ε<1requiresn≥ceil(logε/logρ).',
'The matrices areP=[[1,0],[0,0]] andQ=[[c²,cs],[cs,s²]]. Multiplication givesPQP=c²P, proving the iterate by induction. Their commutator is[[0,cs],[−cs,0]]. Its transpose times itself is c²s²I, so both singular values are|cs| and the norm formula follows. Since0<ρ<1, taking logarithms ofρ^n≤εand dividing by negative logρreverses the inequality, giving the exact ceiling. Asθ→0the commutator tends to zero butρ→1and convergence becomes slow. Asθ→π/2the commutator again tends to zero butρ→0and one cycle annihilates thePline. Thus small commutator alone cannot distinguish near-coincident from near-orthogonal subspaces or predict a unique convergence rate.',
'Takeθ=π/3andε=1/100.',
'ρ=1/4and||[P,Q]||=√3/4. Three cycles leave1/64>1/100, whilefour leave1/256<1/100, so the exact count isfour. Atθ=π/6the commutator norm is the same, butρ=3/4andthe required count is seventeen, demonstrating nonidentifiability from the commutator norm alone.',
'The source constants are exact and their different roles are explicit.',
'The theorem applies to these orthogonal linear projections. Arbitrary nonlinear closure maps need separate contraction and fixed-point assumptions, and floating residual floors affect practical stopping.',
'Generalize to two subspaces using principal angles, then identify the Friedrichs angle controlling convergence after removing the intersection.',['Convex optimization'])

add('Finite closure iteration terminates by rank, but noncommuting closures can hide new elements','2026-06-25T20:00:59Z','Order-theoretic closure','What exact theorem governs a monotone closure ladder, and why does a single ordered pass fail?',
'The late-June source names closure, deficit, idempotence and order defects. A finite-set model supplies a genuine termination theorem, a telescoping deficit identity and an explicit failure of commuting intuition.',
['On the power set of a finite universeU, a closure operatorc is extensive,S⊆cS; monotone,S⊆T⇒cS⊆cT; andidempotent,c²=c.','For two closuresc,diterateS_{n+1}=c(d(S_n)). Define the counting deficit of a step as|S_{n+1}|−|S_n|.'],
'The iteration stabilizes after at most|U|−|S₀|strict increases. Its limit is the least set containingS₀fixed by bothc andd. The sum of all step deficits is the final cardinality increase. A single passcdneed not itself be idempotent.',
'Extensivity givesS_n⊆dS_n⊆c(dS_n)=S_{n+1}. Every strict increase adds an element of the finite universe, proving termination. At a fixed pointS=c(dS), the inclusionsS⊆dS⊆c(dS)=Sforce dS=S; then cS=Saswell. IfTcontainsS₀andis fixed by both, monotonicity inductively givesS_n⊆T, proving leastness of the limit. The deficit sum telescopes because the successive intermediate cardinalities cancel. For nonidempotence of one pass, letU={a,b,c}, letcaddbwhena is present, andletdaddcwhenb is present. Both are closure operators. Starting at{a},cdgives{a,b}, whilea second pass gives{a,b,c}. In the opposite orderdc, thefirstpassalreadyadds both. The ordered composition therefore records actual causal dependency between closure rules.',
'Use the three-element example and count deficits.',
'Forcdthe deficits areone,one,zero. Fordcthe first deficit istwoandthe nextzero. The total gain istwoin either case, butthe intermediate result and stopping time differ. Ifa rule is not monotone, neither the least-common-fixed-point proof nor the finite-increase argument necessarily applies.',
'The closure ladder gains an exact completion and order-defect theorem.',
'A cardinality deficit is not automatically entropy, approximation error or computational cost. Infinite universes require an ordinal or convergence argument and may not stabilize in finitely many steps.',
'Add explicit rule costs and prove a scheduling theorem for a finite implication system, comparing work-list evaluation with repeated global passes while preserving the same least fixed point.',['Convex optimization'])

add('Lossless reconstruction forces injectivity and gives a counting lower bound on compression','rho_x(E_x(x))','Information and recoverability','What mathematical condition makes an encoding reversible, and how do side information and collisions affect a claimed compression gain?',
'The late-June corpus includes recoverabilityρ_x(E_x(x))=x, structural encodings and byte gaps. We extract the exact information constraint behind a lawful code. A reconstruction map depending secretly on the original input can otherwise make the equation vacuous.',
['LetSbe a finite input set,E:S→{0,1}*an encoder andD a single fixed decoder. Lossless meansD(E(x))=xfor everyx∈S. If side informations is used, charge its representation and requireD(s,E_s(x))=x.','For a fixed lengthbthere are2^bcodewords. For lengths at mostbthere are2^{b+1}−1binary strings, including the empty string.'],
'A lossless encoder is injective. A fixed-length lossless code forNinputs needsb≥ceil(log₂N). No injective binary encoder can strictly shorten every binary string of lengths at mostb. The exact source byte gaps can be audited arithmetically but do not prove universality of a compression model.',
'IfE(x)=E(y), applyingthe same decoder givesx=y, proving injectivity. Counting the availableb-bitwords proves the fixed-length bound. For the shortening claim, the input set has2^{b+1}−1strings, while all possible outputs of length at mostb−1number2^b−1, fewer than the inputs. Pigeonhole then forces a collision. Allowing a different decoderD_xfor every originalxdefeats the argument only by hidingxinside the decoder; charging that side information restores a genuine code. Prefix-free variable-length coding adds Kraft’s inequality and permits a distribution-dependent expected-length theorem, but it still cannot shorten every possible input. Thus format-aware gains are real finite dataset properties, not contradictions of incompressibility.',
'Audit the source sizes191821,187496,183184and150517bytes.',
'The first improvement is4325bytes, the next4312bytes. The remaining gap after the latter is32667bytes. These exact differences say nothing about an unseen corpus unless its distribution and fixed decoding method are specified. For a toy set of five inputs a three-bit fixed code suffices, whiletwo bits cannot encode them injectively.',
'The source’s reversible closure requirement becomes a precise injectivity and accounting theorem.',
'No reported compressed file is decoded or benchmarked here. Kolmogorov complexity is uncomputable in general, so a claimed exact minimal representation length needs a finite restricted code family.',
'For the actual codec, give a fixed decoder, encode all model metadata and benchmark total lengths on a held-out corpus, with exact round-trip tests and a clearly defined comparison class.',['Quantum information'])

add('Read-write footprint independence gives a sufficient commutation certificate','conflict graph','Discrete concurrency mathematics','When do two deterministic state updates commute, and why is a footprint graph only a sufficient model?',
'The late-June runtime source gives conflict graphs from mutation and admission footprints. We formulate a finite state theorem that explains the legitimate mathematical role of those sets. It does not infer successful execution or freshness from a diagram alone.',
['A state is a functionσ:V→D on finitely many variables. An actionAreads onlyR_A⊆Vandchanges onlyW_A⊆V: its changed values depend solely onσ restricted toR_A.','Two actions satisfy the symmetric noninterference conditionsW_A∩(R_B∪W_B)=∅andW_B∩(R_A∪W_A)=∅. An admission condition may additionally require selected variables to retain specified values.'],
'Under these conditions,A∘B=B∘Aon every state. A plan of pairwise noninterfering actions can be reordered arbitrarily without changing the final state. The conditions are sufficient rather than necessary.',
'BecauseBdoes not write any variable inR_A, actionAsees the same read values before and afterB; similarlyBsees unchanged read values throughA. Their write sets are disjoint, so neither overwrites the other’s outputs. OnW_Aeach order therefore gives the sameAvalue, onW_Bthe sameBvalue, and outside the union the original value remains. This proves commutation coordinatewise. Any permutation is a product of adjacent swaps, so pairwise commutation proves arbitrary reordering by induction on swaps. Necessity fails, for example, when two actions both set the same variable to the same constant: their write sets overlap but their maps commute. Converselydisjointwrite sets alone are insufficient because one may read a value the other changes.',
'LetAsetx:=y+1andBsetz:=2w, with variablesx,y,z,wdistinct.',
'The write sets{x}and{z}are disjoint from both other read sets, so the actions commute. IfBinsteadsetsy:=0, thenAafterBgivesx=1, whileBafterAretainsx=y_old+1; they need not commute. A receipt basedonyis invalidated bythatwriteunless its admission condition explicitly tolerates the change.',
'The conflict graph has a proved noninterference meaning and a sharp counterexample to a weaker rule.',
'External effects, nondeterminism, aliasing and incomplete read sets can invalidate the model. A graph with omitted dependencies is not a proof of serializability or correctness.',
'For a typed state machine, verify the footprints from action semantics and compute independent-set batches, then include versioned admission predicates to prove each action’s preconditions remain valid.',['Convex optimization'])

add('Tangram areas and chirality give exact invariants but do not classify all assemblies','Tangram mathematics','Euclidean dissection geometry','Which exact arithmetic invariants constrain a tangram assembly, and why does a parallelogram require a reflection to reverse its handedness?',
'The source lists classical tangram areas,√2coordinates and a parallelogram flip. We prove the area accounting and a simple orientation invariant. The claimed total number of convex configurations is left as an external classification, not inferred from these necessary conditions.',
['Normalize the square tangram to areaone. Its pieces aretwo large triangles ofarea1/4,one medium triangle ofarea1/8,two small triangles ofarea1/16,one square ofarea1/8andone parallelogram ofarea1/8.','An assembly uses rigid translations and rotations of thepieces with disjoint interiors. For an ordered pair of nonparallel edge vectorsu,v, oriented area isdet(u,v); rotation preserves its sign and reflection reverses it.'],
'The seven piece areas sum toone, so every lawful assembly has areaone. A labeled nonrectangular parallelogram and its mirror cannot be carried to each other by an orientation-preserving rigid map that respects the two labeled edge directions.',
'Finite additivity of planar area on sets with disjoint interiors gives the first statement. The piece sum is2(1/4)+1/8+2(1/16)+1/8+1/8=1. For the second, any plane orientation-preserving rigid map has linear partRwithdetR=1, so det(Ru,Rv)=det(u,v). A mirror has linear partJwithdetJ=−1and reverses the sign. If the ordered labeled edge vectors have nonzero determinant, these signs cannot agree under a rotation. In an unlabeled polygon extra edge permutations can remove some apparent distinctions, so the exact chirality claim must retain its edge or face labeling. A physical flip permits a reflection and reverses that orientation. Coordinate calculations may be performed inQ(√2)because standard tangram cuts use diagonal directions and forty-five-degree rotations.',
'Useu=(1,0),v=(1,1)for a labeled parallelogram and reflect across thehorizontalaxis.',
'The oriented area changes fromone to−one, whileordinaryarea remainsone. Translation oranyrotationkeeps the positive sign. For an assembly, matching total area is onlynecessary: two equal-area outlines may have incompatible edge lengths or angle combinations, so area alone cannot prove tileability.',
'The dissection model has exact conservation and an explicit orientation boundary.',
'No count of thirteen convex tangrams or6500problems is proved here. Those are classification or catalog claims requiring their own source and exhaustive argument. The chirality statement is explicitly labeled-edge, avoiding a false universal assertion about unlabeled parallelograms.',
'Encode each piece inQ(√2), impose exact edge-contact and nonoverlap constraints and produce a finite assembly certificate before attempting an exhaustive convex-outline classification.',['Convex optimization'])

add('Finite symplectic group order gives the reported projective denominator, not a magic-state proof','PSp(4,5)','Finite group actions','Which exact order formula explains4680000and what can an orbit certificate actually decide?',
'The source reports a projective braid-image denominator and finite resource orbits. We prove the group order in its standard finite symplectic model and state a constructive orbit-membership procedure. The conclusion remains conditional on the actual representation image being that group.',
['LetV=F_q⁴with a nondegenerate alternating form, qodd. Sp(4,q)preserves the form, andPSp(4,q)quotients byits scalar center{±I}.','For a finite matrix groupGgenerated byexplicit matrices and a projective target lineℓ, its orbit is{gℓ:g∈G}; membership requires comparison modulo nonzero scalars.'],
'|Sp(4,q)|=q⁴(q²−1)(q⁴−1), so|PSp(4,5)|=4680000. Exhaustive orbit closure decides membership for a supplied finite action, but being outside one declared orbit does not by itself certify an operational magic resource.',
'Count ordered symplectic bases(e₁,f₁,e₂,f₂). Choosee₁nonzero inq⁴−1ways. The equation⟨e₁,f₁⟩=1is an affine hyperplane withq³solutions. Their span is a nondegenerate plane; its orthogonal complement is a nondegenerate two-plane. Choosee₂nonzero there inq²−1ways, andf₂withpairingoneinqways. The product is(q⁴−1)q³(q²−1)q=q⁴(q²−1)(q⁴−1). Every symplectic matrix is uniquely determined bytheimage ofa fixed symplectic basis, proving the order formula. Foroddqthe scalar symplectic matrices are±Iandthe standard center is exactlythese; quotienting divides bytwo. Atq=5this gives625·24·624/2=4680000. A breadth-first closure under generators and their inverses terminates because the group or its projective action is finite, giving an exact orbit certificate when all operations are overthedeclared field.',
'Compare raw event counts with orbit membership.',
'Many branch events can land on the same projective line, so3864eventsand388targets are different counts. A target outside a braid orbit might still be unavailable, unnormalizable, leakage-prone or reachable by an allowed operation omitted fromthegroup. A genuine operational resource claim needs the allowed free operations and deterministic or probabilistic conversion rules.',
'The denominator is a proved finite-group count with a clear orbit-theorem scope.',
'The braid-image identification and source orbit enumeration are not rerun without their matrices. No outsider, non-braid gate or magic-state certificate is inferred fromtheorder alone.',
'Provide exact projective generators and the complete allowed instrument set, certify orbit closure and then prove a conversion monotone that separates any claimed resource fromall free operations.',['Galois theory','Quantum information'])

add('The recovered seven-crossing quintic admits an exact field and Hessian inverse certificate','exact field','Computational number fields','Can the supplied quintic and Hessian element be certified algebraically without accepting a knot-asymptotic identification?',
'The final sweep supplies a7₂quintic, a reduced Hessian element and a torsion expression. We prove irreducibility and invert the displayed Hessian exactly. These are useful constructive field calculations; identifying them as the correct geometric Hessian remains a separate source-dependent theorem.',
['Letf(y)=y⁵−2y⁴+3y³+2y²−2y−1 andK=Q[y]/(f), withαthe residue ofy. Let h=5+68α+49α²−29α³+21α⁴.','Every field element has a unique representative of degree at most four. Multiplication byhhas a5-by5rational matrix in the basis1,α,…,α⁴, and its determinant is the normN_{K/Q}(h).'],
'f is irreducible overQ. The nonzero elementhhas norm39681=9·4409 and inverse(−1379+1939α−2235α²+2484α³+464α⁴)/39681.',
'Modulo two,fbecomesg=y⁵+y³+1. It has no root inF₂. The only irreducible quadratic isy²+y+1; reducingggivesy+1. The only irreducible cubics arey³+y+1andy³+y²+1; their remainders are respectivelyy²+1andy²+y+1. Thusghas no irreducible factor of degree one,two or three. Any proper factorization of a degree-five polynomial has a factor of degree at most two, so these checks already suffice; the cubic checks provide an independent surplus check. Thereforegis irreducible, and Gauss’s lemma gives irreducibility overQ. InKreduceα⁵=2α⁴−3α³−2α²+2α+1. Repeated multiplication produces the exact matrix ofh. Rational elimination gives determinant39681. Multiplyinghby the stated inverse numerator and reducing by the same relation gives the constant39681with all four higher coefficients zero, independently certifying the inverse. Sincehhas nonzero representative of degree belowfive, it is nonzero in the field even before computing its norm.',
'Compare inverse denominators with the source torsion denominator4409.',
'The Hessian inverse has common denominator39681, exactlynine times4409. The coefficient276/4409is the reduced version of2484/39681. This exact arithmetic relationship does not derive the source torsion formula: that element requires its own defining identity and normalization.',
'The source quintic and one important nondegeneracy calculation are now fully checkable.',
'No identification of this field with a knot trace field, no orientation sign and no thimble coefficient is established by polynomial arithmetic alone. The provided torsion numerator is retained only as historical context.',
'Construct the actual critical-point equations, reduce their Jacobian determinant and torsion identities in this field, then track each complex embedding and the associated contour orientation separately.',['Number fields'])

